.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS VOLATILE
.IP
.EX
#define VOLATILE
.EE
.PP
Marks an object as volatile, telling the compiler that its value may
change by means outside the visible flow of the program, so every read
and write written in the source must actually be performed.
.PP
Typical reasons an object needs it:
.IP \(bu 2
It is written by generated code the compiler cannot see
.IP \(bu 2
It is written by another thread, a signal handler or an interrupt
.IP \(bu 2
It is a memory\-mapped hardware register
.IP \(bu 2
It is modified across \f[CR]setjmp\f[R] / \f[CR]longjmp\f[R]
.PP
When unsupported, \f[CR]VOLATILE\f[R] safely degrades to a no\-op,
preserving source compatibility without altering behavior.
.PP
\f[B]/!\(rs\f[R] \- This is the part that is worth reading twice,
because both spellings compile and only one of them does what is usually
wanted.
.PP
The qualifier binds to whatever sits on its \f[B]left\f[R], or to the
thing on its right when there is nothing on its left:
.IP
.EX
VOLATILE void   *a; \f[I]/* POINTER TO VOLATILE DATA \- THE POINTER ITSELF IS NOT */\f[R]
void            *VOLATILE b; \f[I]/* VOLATILE POINTER \- THE POINTED DATA IS NOT */\f[R]
VOLATILE void   *VOLATILE c; \f[I]/* BOTH */\f[R]
.EE
.PP
To make the \f[B]pointer variable\f[R] itself volatile, the qualifier
must come after the \f[CR]*\f[R].
.PP
There is one convenient exception.
When the pointer type is hidden behind a \f[CR]typedef\f[R], the
qualifier applies to the pointer, which is the intuitive result:
.IP
.EX
\f[B]typedef\f[R] void    *PTR;

VOLATILE PTR    p; \f[I]/* SAME AS: void *VOLATILE p; */\f[R]
.EE
.PP
So \f[CR]VOLATILE PTR\f[R] is a volatile pointer, while hand\-expanding
that typedef into \f[CR]VOLATILE void *\f[R] silently flips the meaning
and qualifies the data instead.
.SS WHY IT MATTERS FOR GENERATED CODE
If a global is written only by runtime\-generated machine code, nothing
in the translation unit ever assigns it as far as the compiler is
concerned.
Under whole\-program visibility the optimizer is then entitled to
conclude the object still holds its initializer and replace every read
with that constant:
.IP
.EX
static PTR  g_this; \f[I]/* NEVER ASSIGNED IN VISIBLE C CODE */\f[R]

void    *connect(void)
{
    \f[B]return\f[R] (g_this); \f[I]/* \-O2 \-> \(dqxorl %eax, %eax\(dq  (FOLDED TO NULL!) */\f[R]
}
.EE
.PP
Adding the qualifier forces the load to happen:
.IP
.EX
static VOLATILE PTR g_this;

void    *connect(void)
{
    \f[B]return\f[R] (g_this); \f[I]/* \-O2 \-> \(dqmovq g_this(%rip), %rax\(dq */\f[R]
}
.EE
.PP
\f[B]NOTE:\f[R] \f[CR]volatile\f[R] orders accesses \f[B]only\f[R] with
respect to other volatile accesses, and it is \f[B]not\f[R] a
synchronization primitive.
It does not provide atomicity and it does not emit CPU memory barriers
(except under MSVC\(aqs \f[CR]/volatile:ms\f[R]).
For sharing mutable data between threads, a real lock or an atomic type
is still required.
.PP
\f[B]Examples\f[R]:
.IP
.EX
VOLATILE int    flag;

\f[B]extern\f[R] VOLATILE unsigned char   *port;
.EE
.IP
.EX
VOLATILE int    counter;

void    tick(void)
{
    counter++;
}
.EE
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS IS__VOLATILE__SUPPORTED
.IP
.EX
#define IS__VOLATILE__SUPPORTED
.EE
.PP
Checks whether the \f[CR]VOLATILE\f[R] macro is defined as non\-empty.
.PP
This indicates that the compiler truly supports the \f[CR]volatile\f[R]
qualifier.
.PP
\f[B]NOTES:\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
The macro is undefined only on pre\-ANSI (K&R) compilers, or when the
user explicitly requested \f[CR]CMT_NO_VOLATILE\f[R].
.IP \(bu 2
Any algorithm whose \f[B]correctness\f[R] depends on the qualifier being
honored must be guarded, since on such a target nothing enforces the
access.
.PP
\f[B]Example\f[R]:
.IP
.EX
#ifdef IS__VOLATILE__SUPPORTED
VOLATILE PTR    g_this;
#else
#error \(dqTHIS MODULE REQUIRES A COMPILER WITH VOLATILE SUPPORT\(dq
#endif
.EE
.SS NOTES
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]GNU\-GCC\f[R] uses the alternate spelling \f[CR]__volatile__\f[R]
on purpose.
Pre\-4.x GCC disables the plain \f[CR]volatile\f[R] under
\f[CR]\-traditional\f[R], while the \f[CR]__\f[R]\-wrapped spelling
keeps working.
It is accepted everywhere the plain keyword is, so it costs nothing on
modern GCC.
Note that \f[CR]\-ansi\f[R] and \f[CR]\-std=c90\f[R] do \f[B]not\f[R]
disable \f[CR]volatile\f[R] \- it is a standard C89 keyword.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]MSVC\f[R] has two modes.
\f[CR]/volatile:ms\f[R] (the default on x86 and x64) adds
acquire/release semantics on top of the standard behavior, while
\f[CR]/volatile:iso\f[R] gives exactly the standard behavior.
The MS mode is strictly stronger, never weaker, so both are safe here.
.IP \(bu 2
Any unknown compiler that announces itself as ANSI C through
\f[CR]__STDC__\f[R] or \f[CR]__STDC_VERSION__\f[R] is trusted without
needing a vendor tag, since the keyword is part of the language by
definition from C89 onward.
.IP \(bu 2
On the degraded path the lowercase \f[CR]volatile\f[R] is also defined
as empty, exactly like \f[CR]KNR_STYLE.H\f[R] does for \f[CR]void\f[R]
and \f[CR]const\f[R].
Without that, any hand\-written \f[CR]volatile\f[R] in user code would
fail to parse on a K&R compiler.
On every supported compiler the lowercase spelling is left completely
untouched, because the native keyword already works and redefining it
would only risk disturbing system headers.
.IP \(bu 2
The qualifier costs nothing when it is not read.
It only suppresses optimization of the accesses to the object it marks,
so marking a hot variable volatile without needing it will pessimize the
surrounding code.
